<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<PathAssistant xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        The BROKER-LISTED disposition path, for the On_Market record type.
        Eight steps: Disposition Readiness, BOV Outreach, Active Listing, Call for Offers,
        LOI, PSA, Closing, Completed.

        Five steps (Disposition Readiness, BOV Outreach, Active Listing, Closing, Completed)
        were copied from the now-deactivated Disposition_Path. FOUR of them are still
        BYTE-IDENTICAL - fieldNames AND info text - and Active Listing is STILL one of them:
        its info was rewritten 2026-08-10 (retraction below), and the IDENTICAL rewrite was
        applied to Disposition_Path in the same change, so the carry-over this comment
        describes did not break.

        🔴 THE ACTIVE LISTING CLOCK IS NO LONGER AN OPEN QUESTION - RETRACTED 2026-08-10 BY
        D27.1 / D28-Q2. The superseded sentences are quoted and retracted rather than deleted,
        because "leave it alone, a question is open" is the exact instruction that left this
        Path contradicting the LWC beside it for a whole tranche:

            RETRACTED: "The Active Listing text is under an OPEN question (the source document
            says ~2 months with a month-1 check, this text says a 6-week clock) and was copied
            forward UNEDITED on purpose. Editing it here would silently pre-empt that
            question."

        WHAT ANSWERED IT: D27.1 - THE DOCUMENT WON. The marketing period is 60 days with a
        30-day traction checkpoint, and traction is logged offers and NOTHING else (D28-Q1;
        "real interest" stays a human judgement made in the internal discussion and is
        deliberately not modelled as a field, so this text must not imply the system can see
        it). THE RECONCILIATION HAS NOW HAPPENED: the Active Listing info below states that
        model, and every band it names is one DispositionTractionService computes - NOT_LISTED,
        ON_TRACK, CHECKPOINT_DUE at day 30, REVIEW_OVERDUE at day 40, HARD_STOP at day 60, all
        short-circuited to ON_TRACK by a single logged offer. That service decides a band
        nowhere else; this text must never state a threshold it does not hold.

        TWO THINGS WERE DROPPED AND MUST NOT COME BACK. "Escalation to Ali" promised a
        notification D9 defers, so nothing sends it - the same stale-promise defect that
        condemned the retired listingAlerts mock. And the week-numbered milestones are BANNED
        STRINGS in lwc/listingAlerts/__tests__/listingAlerts.test.js, which asserts the sibling
        traction panel never renders them; a Path rendering them next to that panel is exactly
        the one-screen contradiction BrokerListingController's header exists to prevent.

        CLOSING IS THE ONE DEVIATION, added 2026-08-09 on the code review's C1 finding. Its
        fieldNames are unchanged and the original two sentences are unchanged; a third
        sentence was APPENDED telling the user to complete the wire verification BEFORE
        submitting the Closing approval. That ordering is not a preference - Closing_Approval
        sets recordEditability AdminOnly, so while it is pending the Disposition is locked and
        Wire_Verification_Rollup cannot write the flag (it now fails soft rather than rolling
        the Wire save back). Signposting the sequence is what stops the user discovering it.
        The IDENTICAL sentence is on Disposition_Path_Off_Market's Closing step - these two
        must stay in step with each other.

        Call for Offers, LOI and PSA are new, text-only steps: none of them has a
        Disposition__c field to surface. PSA_Executed__c deliberately STAYS on the Closing
        step where it is today - moving it to the new PSA step is defensible but changes
        deployed behaviour for no requested benefit.

        Step ORDER in this file is the design's path order for readability. Runtime display
        order comes from the Disposition_Stage__c picklist value order (sorted=false), not
        from this file, so a retrieve will re-serialise these alphabetically.
    -->
    <active>true</active>
    <entityName>Disposition__c</entityName>
    <fieldName>Disposition_Stage__c</fieldName>
    <masterLabel>Disposition Path On Market</masterLabel>
    <pathAssistantSteps>
        <fieldNames>Property_Asset__c</fieldNames>
        <info>Confirm the asset metrics and Argus signal support the sell decision before advancing to BOV Outreach.</info>
        <picklistValueName>Disposition Readiness</picklistValueName>
    </pathAssistantSteps>
    <pathAssistantSteps>
        <fieldNames>Package_Sent__c</fieldNames>
        <fieldNames>Submission_Deadline__c</fieldNames>
        <info>Send OM packages to 5-6 brokers. Matrix auto-generates when 3+ BOV responses are received.</info>
        <picklistValueName>BOV Outreach</picklistValueName>
    </pathAssistantSteps>
    <pathAssistantSteps>
        <fieldNames>Selected_Broker__c</fieldNames>
        <info>The 60-day marketing clock runs from the Listing Date on this Disposition and does not start until that date is set. Traction means logged offers and nothing else. With none: day 30 is the traction checkpoint, where the team discusses internally and decides whether to change the broker; day 40 is review overdue; day 60 is Hard Stop. A single logged offer clears the checkpoint at any point.</info>
        <picklistValueName>Active Listing</picklistValueName>
    </pathAssistantSteps>
    <pathAssistantSteps>
        <info>Best-and-final round. The call-for-offers date is tracked on the Broker Listing record (Call For Offers Date), not on the Disposition, so it cannot be shown as a Path field here - open the related Broker Listing to set it. Offers arriving in this round are captured as Disposition Offer records.</info>
        <picklistValueName>Call for Offers</picklistValueName>
    </pathAssistantSteps>
    <pathAssistantSteps>
        <info>LOI is a phase of this disposition, not a separate record - the disposition-side LOI record arrives in a later change. Until then the negotiation stays on the Disposition Offer record: drive it through Offer Status and the DPEG Counter Price, DPEG Counter Date, Buyer Counter Price and Final Agreed Price fields.</info>
        <picklistValueName>LOI</picklistValueName>
    </pathAssistantSteps>
    <pathAssistantSteps>
        <info>PSA is a phase of this disposition, not a separate record - the disposition-side contract has no record yet and arrives in a later change. PSA Executed on this Disposition is the only marker; tick it on the Closing step once the purchase and sale agreement is signed.</info>
        <picklistValueName>PSA</picklistValueName>
    </pathAssistantSteps>
    <pathAssistantSteps>
        <fieldNames>PSA_Executed__c</fieldNames>
        <fieldNames>Title_Company__c</fieldNames>
        <info>PSA Executed = TRUE spawns closing tasks. Wire fraud prevention gate (6 fields) must be complete before wiring. Complete the wire verification BEFORE submitting the Closing approval - the approval locks this Disposition, so a Wire saved while it is pending will not update the Wire Verification Completed flag until the approval resolves.</info>
        <picklistValueName>Closing</picklistValueName>
    </pathAssistantSteps>
    <pathAssistantSteps>
        <fieldNames>Closing_Date__c</fieldNames>
        <fieldNames>Net_Sale_Proceeds__c</fieldNames>
        <info>Deal closed. Confirm the closing date, net sale proceeds, and that the closing statement is on file.</info>
        <picklistValueName>Completed</picklistValueName>
    </pathAssistantSteps>
    <recordTypeName>On_Market</recordTypeName>
</PathAssistant>
